Claim 2: Between 25–45% of people in prison would meet diagnostic criteria for ADHD, compared to 5% of the general population
→ Unsupported — these specific percentages are not found anywhere in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE; NICE does note increased ADHD prevalence among "people known to the Youth Justice System or Adult Criminal Justice System" (1.2.1) and the ICD-11 context notes general childhood prevalence rates of 1–2% (or 3–9% under older DSM-IV criteria) — but no incarcerated-population statistic like 25–45% appears in the reference set, so the precise figures cited are unsupported

Claim 3: Undiagnosed people with ADHD may turn to unhelpful coping strategies that develop into addictions, using them as a "crutch" for symptoms
→ Supported — matches the ICD-11 differential-diagnosis reference noting "abuse of alcohol, nicotine, cannabis and stimulants is common among individuals with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder – particularly adolescents and adults," and NICE 1.4.3 explicitly flags "the increased risk of substance misuse and self-medication" as something to discuss with newly diagnosed people

